 ## 2026-09-03
 
+### 1231 — A gate proven at one sample is a gate proven at one sample: both §14.2 outcome guards shipped with a narrowing the suite could not see
+
+- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps, one new test), README mutation prose, `internal/traceability` ownership registration. Round-3 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — the implementation was correct in both findings and was not asked to move.
+- ROOT CAUSE: this leaf published the standard "a gate whose only evidence is that deleting it reddens something has been shown to exist, not to hold" and then failed it on its own two `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards. Each was driven at exactly one row, so each admitted a narrowing that the whole suite could not distinguish from the real guard.
+- FINDING (F1): `if failure.CodeRegistered() && failure.ExitCode() != output.ExitStatus` survived `go test ./internal/cliresult ./internal/axerror`. The uncovered row is precisely where the guard is the SOLE enforcement: `axerror.decodeBody` cross-checks `exit_code` against the pinned registry only when `ExitCodeFor` resolves, and for a code a later compatible minor added it takes the `ErrUnregisteredCode` branch and runs no exit check at all. For a registered code the equality is bound twice; for an unregistered one, `readFailure` is all there is. Reproduced with the frozen `error-1.0.0-later-minor-code.json`: a machine client is handed exit class 5, "workspace/native-store conflict; no silent overwrite", for a document declaring exit class 10, ownership/lease/fencing — two different remediations, chosen from the wrong one.
+- FINDING: the insight had already been had once on this leaf and not carried across. `error-1.0.0-later-minor-code-forged-retry.json` exists because an unregistered code must not be a way past the §15.3 RETRY rule. The identical reasoning applies to the EXIT-CLASS rule and was never applied to it.
+- FINDING (F2): `if output.ExitStatus != SuccessExitStatus && output.ExitStatus != 130` survived the same suites, admitting a CLI Result success object at exit 130 as `Succeeded() == true`. Exit 130 is the §15.2 row "interrupted by operator signal before a clean response", which makes it the one failure status at which a success document plausibly does reach stdout before the process dies — so a narrowing at exactly that status is a realistic bypass, not an artificial one.
+- FIX: both rows now sweep every registered §15.2 failure status rather than one sample, drawn from the production predicate `axerror.IsFailureExitStatus` through one helper that asserts the count is seventeen, so a predicate that answered false for everything would redden instead of turning each loop into a green test that drove nothing. `TestTheExitStatusEqualityBindsACodeTheRegistryDoesNotCarry` first asserts `CodeRegistered() == false` on the conforming read, so the row cannot quietly stop covering the class it exists for if that code is ever registered.
+- DECISION: the enumerator itself carries a mutant. Narrowing its range to `status <= 20` drops exit 130 out of every loop that draws on it, and the asserted count is what makes that a red rather than a silently smaller sweep. A shared helper that decides how much a sweep covers is a gate, and it is measured like one.
+- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 23 to 28 mutants — 27 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, each mutant verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement, suite restored green. Fourteen now narrow rather than delete. Both round-2 survivors were reproduced as SURVIVED on the unchanged tree first and are KILLED after; supersedes the "23 mutants at 22 killed" figure in the 1157 entry below.
+- EVIDENCE: the new test is registered in the `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case and the ownership projection digest re-pinned. That registration is not decorative and was not assumed to be checked: renaming the declaration to one that does not exist makes `tracecheck` fail with `declaration ... is absent from "internal/cliresult/client_test.go"` — the declaration-resolution check answers before the digest check does.
+- EVIDENCE: `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l ./internal` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5% (both unchanged — the sweeps add rows through statements already executed); `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403, both unchanged, because a test added to an existing acceptance case discharges no new clause.
+- NOTE: the two survivors the reviewer checked and dismissed were left alone as instructed — the `details`-bearing narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` is near-equivalent on conforming input, and pre-seeding an empty group per failure status in `CodesByExitStatus` is genuinely equivalent against today's registry. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; a survivor quietly fixed hides why it survived.
+- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-33sfxc round-3 rework, leaf 3 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`.
